\section{Summarize a Subtree by the Shapes It Matches}

A bottom-up matcher need not try every instruction pattern from scratch at every node. In ``Algorithms for Linear-Time, Tree-Based Pattern Matching,'' Blindell describes Hoffmann and O'Donnell's scheme: assign each node a label computed from its operator and the labels of its ordered children. A label identifies a \emph{matchset} \cite[section~3.6.1]{blindell2016instruction}. This gives a deterministic bottom-up tree automaton: for each symbol $f$ of arity $k$, a transition $\delta_f(q_1,\ldots,q_k)$ returns the parent state. A nullary operator has a transition with no arguments. This is not an LR stack over a serialized tree; each label is computed from the completed child subtrees.

For a simple construction, enumerate the distinct subpatterns of all the instruction patterns, including a wildcard $*$ that accepts any subtree. A state records the subset that matches at its root. Include proper subpatterns even when they have no instruction attached: a parent may need to know whether a particular shape matched its child. For every subpattern $f(p_1,\ldots,p_k)$, set its bit exactly when each $p_i$ is present in the state of child $i$. Set the wildcard bit unconditionally. Build transitions for reachable state tuples by interning equal bitsets. A transition producing a genuinely new state means that more tuples may now be reachable. The construction stops because the bitsets range over a finite family.

\section{Recognize Two Independent Patterns}

Take ordered, fixed-width wrapping integer operations, with a nullary temporary $\mathrm{tmp}$, unary $\mathrm{neg}$, and binary $\mathrm{mul}$ and $\mathrm{add}$. The machine description advertises ordinary addition $A$, multiply--add $P$, and subtraction $Q$ as candidates:

\[
\begin{array}{rcl@{\qquad}rcl}
 A&=&\mathrm{add}(*,*) &
 M&=&\mathrm{mul}(*,*)\\
 P&=&\mathrm{add}(M,*) &
 N&=&\mathrm{neg}(*)\\
 Q&=&\mathrm{add}(*,N). &&
\end{array}
\]

$M$ and $N$ are auxiliary subpatterns, not reported instructions in this grammar fragment. The $\mathrm{add}$ transition needs only one bit from each child, namely $M$ from the left and $N$ from the right. Omitting the wildcard bit, which is always set, its entire table after projecting to those bits is

\begin{center}
\begin{tabular}{c|cc}
 & Right lacks $N$ & Right has $N$ \\
\hline
Left lacks $M$ & $\{A\}$ & $\{A,Q\}$ \\
Left has $M$ & $\{A,P\}$ & $\{A,P,Q\}$
\end{tabular}
\end{center}

$P$ and $Q$ overlap without either implying the other. This is the independent-pattern case that matters in Blindell's account: an encoding that remembers only a single most specific pattern is not sufficient in general. In this example there is no priority order that lets us recover the three possible nonempty subsets of $\{P,Q\}$ from the identity of one maximal match alone. This is a correctness issue before it is a speed issue: omitting a match could remove the eventual best cover.

% Annotated walkthrough
\section{Label an Overlap in Postorder}

Run the automaton on $\mathrm{add}(\mathrm{mul}(a,b),\mathrm{neg}(c))$, where $a,b,c$ are temporary leaves. Again suppress the wildcard bit in the displayed states.

\begin{verbatim}
input occurrence          ordered child states       state
tmp(a), tmp(b), tmp(c)     ()                         {}
mul(a,b)                  ({}, {})                   {M}       [1]
neg(c)                    ({})                       {N}
add(mul(a,b),neg(c))       ({M}, {N})                 {A,P,Q}   [2]
\end{verbatim}

\noindent\textbf{[1]} The empty display for a temporary means that it matches only the wildcard. That is enough to satisfy both children of $M$. The output of this transition matters to the parent even though $M$ itself carries no instruction action.

\noindent\textbf{[2]} The left and right tests are both true. A report for $P$ places frontier holes at $a$, $b$, and the whole $\mathrm{neg}(c)$ subtree; $Q$ instead leaves holes at the whole $\mathrm{mul}(a,b)$ subtree and at $c$. These are alternative tiles at the same node, not a plan to execute both. If the children are swapped, the table gives $\{A\}$: commutativity of the underlying arithmetic does not make the ordered transition symmetric.

The labeling invariant follows by induction on subtree height. The wildcard bit needs no hypotheses. Every other bit tests the current operator and one correct child-state bit for each of its child patterns. This is precisely when the corresponding shape matches. The proof establishes neither that the frontier goals can be supplied nor that a particular match is cheap.

\section{The Cost of the Tables}

The labeled tree in \Cref{fig:visual-266} makes the two independent witnesses visible: \(M\) in the left child enables \(P\), and \(N\) in the right enables \(Q\).

