With fixed arities and constant-time table lookup, labeling takes one transition per IR node, plus the cost of listing the matches if the caller wants them enumerated. Dense storage for a $k$-ary operator can nevertheless use $|\mathcal{Q}|^k$ cells for state set $\mathcal{Q}$. Blindell discusses Chase's compression of identical rows and columns \cite[section~3.6.1]{blindell2016instruction}; the two membership tests in the $\mathrm{add}$ table above are an explicit instance of using smaller classes for each argument position.

This finite construction handles structural wildcards independently. If a rule requires two frontier occurrences to name the same concrete temporary, the shape table can nominate it but cannot certify equality from those wildcard bits. An implementation can check such payload predicates on candidates, or refine symbols for a finite relevant classification, such as a literal's encodable range. Blindell also discusses finite tree automata that solve selection as well as matching \cite[section~3.7.2]{blindell2016instruction}; the automaton here intentionally stops at shape recognition. Chapter~\ref{ch:ext-267} shows how to choose among the available candidates.
